Advanced Concepts and Technical Terms
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1. PRIMING VALUE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
What is it?
    A starting number you set before a loop or recursion begins.
    Example: to add numbers, you start with sum = 0 (the priming value).
    To multiply, you start with product = 1.

Analogy:
    Before counting apples, you set the basket count to zero. That zero is the priming value.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
2. UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR (UB)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
What is it?
    When your code does something the C language rules do not allow.
    The computer may do anything – crash, give wrong results, or even work by
    accident. You should never rely on it.

Examples:
    • Using a variable before giving it a value.
    • Reading beyond the end of an array.
    • Dividing by zero.

Analogy:
    A recipe says "bake for 20 minutes". If you bake for 2 hours, you get charcoal –
    but the recipe didn't say what happens. That's undefined.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
3. IMPLEMENTATION‑DEFINED BEHAVIOUR
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
What is it?
    The C standard says “the compiler must choose one way and tell you in its
    manual”. It is not random – it is just different on different computers.

Examples:
    • How many bytes an int has (usually 4, but it can be 2 on some machines).
    • Whether a plain char is signed or unsigned.

Analogy:
    Different car brands have different fuel tank sizes. The size is decided by the
    manufacturer, but they always tell you in the owner’s manual.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
4. SEQUENCE POINTS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
What is it?
    A point in your code where everything before it is completely finished.
    At a sequence point, all side effects (like updating a variable) are done.

Why it matters:
    If you change the same variable twice without a sequence point between them,
    you get undefined behaviour (trouble).

Example of a sequence point: the semicolon at the end of a full statement.
    int a = 5;  // sequence point here

Analogy:
    A traffic light. Only after the light turns green, the next car can move.
    A sequence point is like that green light for operations.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
5. STRICT ALIASING RULE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
What is it?
    In simple terms: “Do not look at a piece of memory as if it were a different
    type than it really is, except when using char or void pointers.”

You break the rule if you:
    Write an int and then read it as if it were a float using a pointer.

Why it exists:
    It lets the compiler optimise your code better.

Safe rule:
    Always use the correct pointer type for the variable you are accessing.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
6. MEMORY MODEL
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Imagine your computer’s memory as a big warehouse divided into areas:

    • Stack   → sticky notes for short‑term tasks (local variables, function calls).
               Automatically thrown away when the task finishes.

    • Heap    → a storage room where you put boxes (dynamic memory).
               You must manually remove them when no longer needed.

    • Data/BSS → whiteboards with permanent information (global variables).

    • Code    → the shelf with the instruction manual (your compiled program).

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
7. DATA TYPES – TYPE QUALIFIERS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    const    → “read‑only”. Once set, you cannot change it.
    volatile → “may change by itself” (e.g., a sensor value).
               Tells the compiler to always read fresh, not from cache.

Analogy:
    const = a laminated card, you can read it but can’t write on it.
    volatile = a live sports score; it can change any moment.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
8. STORAGE CLASSES
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    auto     → normal local variable. Automatically created and destroyed.
    register → a hint to keep the variable in super‑fast CPU memory. (No guarantee.)
    static   → lives for the whole program run. Inside a function it remembers its
               value between calls. Outside functions, it is private to that file.
    extern   → “this variable exists somewhere else”. Used to share variables
               between different .c files.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
9. TYPEDEF
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Creates a nickname for a data type.
    Example: typedef unsigned long ulong;   // now 'ulong' means unsigned long.

Analogy:
    Instead of saying “Mister Bartholomew the Third” every time, you call him “Bart”.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
10. STRUCTURES (struct)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    A way to group different pieces of information together.
    Example: a Student has a name and an age.
        struct Student {
            char name[50];
            int age;
        };
    Now you can create a Student variable that holds both.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
11. UNION (union)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Like a struct, but all members share the same memory spot.
    Only one member holds a valid value at a time.

Analogy:
    A whiteboard that can hold either a shopping list or a to‑do list, but not both
    at the same time. You erase one before using the other.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
12. BIT‑FIELDS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    You can tell a struct member to use only a few bits, not a whole byte.
    Example: storing a flag (yes/no) uses just 1 bit instead of 8.

    struct {
        unsigned int isOn : 1;
        unsigned int mode : 3;
    } status;

This saves memory when you have many small true/false settings.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
13. FLEXIBLE ARRAY MEMBER
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    The last member of a struct can be an array with no fixed size.
    You decide its size when you allocate memory.
    Example: a network packet with a header and then some data bytes.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
14. POINTERS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    A pointer stores an address (like a house number).
    & gives the address of a variable.
    * follows the pointer to the value at that address.

Analogy:
    A library card (pointer) doesn’t hold the book; it tells you where the book is
    on the shelf.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
15. NULL POINTER
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    A special pointer value that means “points to nothing”.
    Always check if a pointer is NULL before using it.

Analogy:
    A blank library card – there is no shelf location written yet.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
16. FUNCTION POINTERS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    A variable that can hold the address of a function.
    You can then call that function using the pointer.
    Useful when you want to choose between different actions at runtime.

Example:
    int (*operation)(int,int);   // operation can point to add or multiply.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
17. DYNAMIC MEMORY (malloc / calloc / realloc / free)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    malloc  → ask the heap for a block of memory (uninitialised).
    calloc  → ask for memory and zero everything.
    realloc → resize a block you already have.
    free    → give the memory back.

Important: Always free what you malloc, or you get a memory leak.

Analogy:
    Renting a storage locker. If you don’t return the keys (free), the locker
    stays locked and nobody else can use it.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
18. MEMORY ALIGNMENT AND PADDING
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    The computer likes to store data at addresses that are multiples of its size
    (like 4‑byte int at addresses 0,4,8,12…). This is alignment.
    Sometimes the compiler adds empty bytes (padding) inside a struct to
    respect this rule. This makes access faster but uses a bit more memory.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
19. ENDIANNESS (BYTE ORDER)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Big‑Endian: like writing a number normally  (most significant digit first).
    Little‑Endian: like writing a number backwards (least significant digit first).

Most PCs use little‑endian. Network data uses big‑endian.
C functions like htons() help you convert.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
20. ARRAYS AND STRINGS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Array: many items of the same type placed next to each other in memory.
    String: a character array that ends with '\0' (the null character).

    String scan sets (%[ ]):
        %[^\n]   – read the whole line including spaces.
        %[0-9]   – read only digits.
        %[^a]    – read until the letter 'a' appears.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
21. PREPROCESSOR
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Runs before the compiler. It handles #include, #define, #ifdef, etc.
    #define PI 3.14   → a simple text replacement.
    Macros can be tricky; prefer const variables and functions when you can.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
22. TYPE CONVERSIONS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Implicit conversion: the compiler automatically changes a small type to a
                        larger one so they can be used together.
    Explicit cast: you force a type change with (type).
    Example: float f = (float) 5;   // 5.0

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
23. RECURSION
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    A function that calls itself.
    Needs a base case (when to stop) or it will call forever and crash.

Analogy:
    Matryoshka dolls. Opening the first doll reveals a smaller one inside, then
    another smaller one, until you reach the tiniest doll (base case).

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
24. TAIL RECURSION
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    A special recursion where the recursive call is the very last thing the
    function does. Some compilers can then run it without growing the stack
    (like converting recursion into a loop).

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
25. STANDARD LIBRARY HEADERS (COMMON ONES)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    <stdio.h>   – printf, scanf, file operations.
    <stdlib.h>  – malloc, free, random numbers.
    <string.h>  – string functions (strlen, strcpy, strcmp).
    <ctype.h>   – check character types (isalpha, isdigit).
    <math.h>    – sin, cos, sqrt.
    <time.h>    – date and time.
    <assert.h>  – for debugging (assert condition).

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
26. FILE I/O
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    fopen  – open a file.
    fprintf/fscanf – write/read formatted text.
    fread/fwrite   – read/write raw bytes.
    fclose – close the file.
    Files can be text ("r","w") or binary ("rb","wb").

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
27. ERROR HANDLING
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    errno  – a variable set by functions when something goes wrong.
    perror – prints a message describing the error.

    assert  – if the condition is false, the program stops and shows an error.
              Good for debugging.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
28. BITWISE OPERATORS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    & (AND)  → keep bits that are 1 in both numbers.
    | (OR)   → set bits to 1 if either number has 1.
    ^ (XOR)  → flip bits that are different.
    ~ (NOT)  → flip all bits.
    << (shift left)  → multiply by 2.
    >> (shift right) → divide by 2.

Very useful for flags, low‑level hardware, and optimisations.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
29. VOLATILE IN DEPTH
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Tell the compiler: “This variable may change outside our code, so always
    read it fresh, never cache it in a register.” Used for hardware registers
    or variables changed by interrupts.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
30. INLINE ASSEMBLY & COMPILER INTRINSICS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Rarely needed for beginners. These let you write assembly language inside C
    or use special functions built into the compiler for extra speed. They are
    not standard and vary between compilers.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
31. C STANDARDS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    • C89 / C90 – the classic C.
    • C99      – added // comments, inline, VLAs, etc.
    • C11      – added threads, atomics, _Generic.
    • C17      – small fixes.
    • C23      – future standard.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
32. COMMAND LINE ARGUMENTS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    int main(int argc, char *argv[])
    argc = how many words were typed.
    argv = the words, split into an array.
    argv[0] is always the program name.

Example:
    ./myprogram hello world
    argc = 3
    argv[0] = "./myprogram", argv[1] = "hello", argv[2] = "world".

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
33. UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR (COMMON PITFALLS TO AVOID)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    • Forgetting to initialise a variable before using it.
    • Writing to array index that is too big.
    • Using a pointer after freeing it.
    • Returning the address of a local variable.
    • Assuming int size is always 4 bytes.
    • Expecting char to be signed (or unsigned) everywhere.

